nvolved, including Daniel I. Thornton and the concept of instruments, contribute to the broader understanding of econometric methodologies.\n\n## Importance of the SARGAN Test in Econometrics\n\nThe SARGAN test is a fundamental tool in econometrics, particularly for assessing the validity of instrumental variables in regression models. It helps ensure that the instruments used are not correlated with the error term, which is crucial for obtaining consistent estimates. The SARGAN statistic is derived from the residuals of a regression model estimated using instrumental variables, testing the over-identifying restrictions when the number of instruments exceeds the number of endogenous variables. A rejection of the null hypothesis indicates potential bias in the IV estimates, highlighting the test's critical role in empirical research. [Data: Entities (2162); Relationships (2784)]\n\n## Connection between SARGAN and Thornton\n\nDaniel I. Thornton is referenced alongside the SARGAN test in the context of econometric testing and methodology. His contributions to the understanding of cointegration and economic modeling complement the SARGAN test's application, providing a broader framework for analyzing economic relationships. The relationship between SARGAN and Thornton underscores the collaborative nature of econometric research, where various methodologies and tests are interlinked to enhance the reliability of economic analyses. [Data: Entities (2529, 2162); Relationships (3221)]\n\n## Role of Instrumental Variables in Econometrics\n\nInstrumental variables (IV) are crucial in econometrics for estimating causal relationships when controlled experiments are not feasible. They help address endogeneity issues by providing a means to isolate the causal effect of an explanatory variable on the dependent variable. The relationship between IV and instruments is foundational, as instruments are specifically designed to account for unobserved confounding in regression models. 